.TH "IDBFACTORY.DELETEDATABASE" "3JS" "September 1, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
IDBFactory.deleteDatabase \- IDBFactory: deleteDatabase() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]deleteDatabase()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]IDBFactory\f[R] interface requests the deletion of a database.
The method returns an \f[CR]IDBOpenDBRequest\f[R] object immediately,
and performs the deletion operation asynchronously.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
// For the current standard:
deleteDatabase(name)

// For the experimental version with \(gaoptions\(ga (see below):
deleteDatabase(name)
deleteDatabase(name, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R]
The name of the database you want to delete.
Note that attempting to delete a database that doesn\(cqt exist does not
throw an exception, in contrast to
\f[CR]IDBDatabase.deleteObjectStore()\f[R], which does throw an
exception if the named object store does not exist.
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R] \f[I](non\-standard)\f[R]
In Gecko, since version 26, you can include a non\-standard optional
storage parameter that specifies whether you want to delete a
\f[CR]permanent\f[R] (the default value) IndexedDB, or an indexedDB in
\f[CR]temporary\f[R] storage (aka shared pool.)
.SS Return value
An \f[CR]IDBOpenDBRequest\f[R] on which subsequent events related to
this request are fired.
.PP
If the operation is successful, the value of the request\(cqs
\f[CR]result\f[R] property is \f[CR]null\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
If the database is successfully deleted, then a \f[CR]success\f[R] event
is fired on the request object returned from
\f[CR]deleteDatabase()\f[R], with its \f[CR]result\f[R] set to
\f[CR]undefined\f[R].
If an error occurs during deletion, an \f[CR]error\f[R] event is fired
on the request object returned from this method.
.PP
When \f[CR]deleteDatabase()\f[R] is called, any other open connections
to this particular database are sent a \f[CR]versionchange\f[R] event,
allowing them to close so that the deletion can proceed.
.PP
If a connection is not closed in response to the
\f[CR]versionchange\f[R] event, the deletion is blocked: the
request\(cqs \f[CR]success\f[R] event does not fire, and a
\f[CR]blocked\f[R] event is fired on the request instead.
The deletion stays pending until every connection to the database is
closed.
.PP
To let it complete, close each connection.
This is typically done by calling \f[CR]IDBDatabase.close()\f[R] from
inside the \f[CR]versionchange\f[R] event handler:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// db is an open connection (e.g. from a previous indexedDB.open() success)\f[R]
db.addEventListener(\(dqversionchange\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  db.close();
});
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] dbDeleteRequest = indexedDB.deleteDatabase(\(dqtoDoList\(dq);

dbDeleteRequest.onerror = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.error(\(dqError deleting database.\(dq);
};

dbDeleteRequest.onsuccess = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqDatabase deleted successfully\(dq);

  console.log(event.result); \f[I]// should be undefined\f[R]
};
.EE
